<interrogationes unitas="matrix">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I build a matrix of integers, rationals or Laurent polynomials?
    <responsum symbolum="matrix_ex_chorda matrix_nulla matrix_identitas matrix_pone ANULUS_INTEGRORUM ANULUS_RATIONALIUM ANULUS_POLYNOMIORUM anulus_residuorum">Pick
      a ring descriptor (&amp;ANULUS_INTEGRORUM for Z with Magnus
      elements, &amp;ANULUS_RATIONALIUM for Q with Fractio,
      &amp;ANULUS_POLYNOMIORUM for Z[t, t^-1] with Polynomium, or
      anulus_residuorum(n, piscina) for Z/n with i32 residues) and either
      parse text with matrix_ex_chorda, e.g. [t - 1, 1; -t, t - 1]
      (commas between entries, semicolons between rows, [] is 0 x 0),
      or start from matrix_nulla / matrix_identitas and fill it with
      matrix_pone. matrix_ad_chordam prints the same format back.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I change an entry of a matrix after I have used it in a computation?
    <responsum symbolum="matrix_pone matrix_elementum">Do
      not: the contract is build then freeze. matrix_pone is meant only
      while you are constructing a matrix; every operation returns a NEW
      matrix and never changes its arguments. matrix_pone copies the
      element struct (it does not deep-copy a Magnus or Polynomium),
      and an index out of range silently does nothing. Read entries
      with matrix_elementum, which returns a pointer to the ring's
      element type, or NIHIL out of range.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I compute a determinant, for example an Alexander polynomial?
    <responsum symbolum="matrix_determinans matrix_transposita matrix_subtrahe matrix_multiplica">matrix_determinans(m,
      piscina, &amp;valor) writes an element of the ring (a Magnus, a
      Fractio or a Polynomium you declare) and returns FALSUM if the
      matrix is not square or the ring is not an integral domain (Z/n
      with n composite: integrum is FALSUM); 0 x 0
      gives 1. It is fraction-free Bareiss elimination, so it works
      exactly over Z and Z[t, t^-1] without fractions. The worklog builds
      det(V - t V^T) from a Seifert matrix with transpose, multiply and
      subtract.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find out why a matrix operation returned FALSUM?
    <responsum symbolum="matrix_adde matrix_multiplica matrix_determinans matrix_forma_hermite">Every
      b32 function returns FALSUM, and leaves its output untouched, when
      dimensions or rings do not match (rings are compared by descriptor
      address), when an element operation refuses (a Polynomium
      exponent out of range, an inexact division), or when the ring
      lacks what the algorithm needs (no integrum for determinant, rank
      and kernel; no Euclidean hooks for Hermite and Smith). There is no
      error code; check those three things.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I destroy the piscina of the input matrix after computing a result from it?
    <responsum symbolum="matrix_determinans matrix_nucleus matrix_multiplica matrix_transposita matrix_adde matrix_subtrahe">Only
      for some results. Determinant, kernel, product and the normal
      forms are always deep-copied into the piscina you pass, so they
      own their memory. matrix_transposita, matrix_adde,
      matrix_subtrahe and matrix_pone copy element structs and may
      SHARE storage with the arguments, so those results stay valid
      only while the arguments' piscinae live.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what kind of basis does matrix_nucleus return, and when do I need matrix_reticulum_nuclei instead?
    <responsum symbolum="matrix_nucleus matrix_reticulum_nuclei">matrix_nucleus
      gives columns spanning the kernel over the fraction field, with
      entries in the ring (they are minors, so their size is bounded),
      but over Z they are not primitive and not a lattice basis: for
      [2, 4] it gives (-4, 2). matrix_reticulum_nuclei (Euclidean rings,
      Z today) gives a basis of the integer kernel lattice, (-2, 1) up
      to sign, so every integer solution is an integer combination of
      its columns. Both have columns minus rank columns.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get the Smith or Hermite normal form with the transformation matrices?
    <responsum symbolum="matrix_forma_smith matrix_forma_hermite">matrix_forma_hermite(a,
      piscina, &amp;h, &amp;u) gives H = U A by row operations (positive
      pivots, entries above reduced, zero rows last);
      matrix_forma_smith(a, piscina, &amp;d, &amp;u, &amp;v) gives
      D = U A V with each d_i dividing the next. Pass NIHIL for u or v
      when you do not need the certificate; it saves time and memory.
      Only rings with divisor_communis, divide_cum_residuo and
      compara_normam work, which today means Z; Q and Z[t, t^-1] return
      FALSUM.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why can the determinant or rank of a Z[t, t^-1] matrix return FALSUM when the answer is well defined?
    <responsum symbolum="matrix_determinans matrix_gradus" via="include/matrix.h lib/matrix.worklog.md">Elimination
      makes intermediate exponents grow: the rank of [t^(2^29), 1; 1,
      t^(2^29)] needs t^(2^30), which is past Polynomium's exponent
      bound, so the element operation refuses and the whole call
      returns FALSUM with the output untouched. Huge sparse exponents
      also need Polynomium's dense memory. This is documented and
      pinned by a test, not a bug to work around.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does matrix create its own scratch piscinae, and how do I measure them?
    <responsum symbolum="matrix_apex_officinarum matrix_determinans matrix_multiplica" via="lib/matrix.worklog.md">Elimination
      produces many intermediate values; they live in four internal
      scratch piscinae (two alternating, one per-entry temporary, one
      stable) that are rolled back as the work proceeds, so only the
      result is deep-copied into your piscina. Determinant, rank and
      kernel skip the scratch when the matrix has at most XXV entries
      AND every entry is stored inline (anulus-&gt;parvum: a Polynomium
      only when it is zero); multiplication uses the same rule with at
      most CXXV element products. Hermite, Smith and the kernel lattice
      always use scratch. A 2 x 2 determinant cost 0.5 us with four
      piscinae against 0.007 us without (worklog A2).
      matrix_apex_officinarum reports the peak scratch bytes of the last
      multiplication, elimination or normal form (0 when that call
      took the small path).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a matrix with zero rows or zero columns round-trip through text?
    <responsum symbolum="matrix_ad_chordam matrix_ex_chorda">No:
      any matrix with a zero dimension (r x 0 or 0 x c) prints as [],
      and [] reads back as 0 x 0, so the dimensions are lost. Keep the
      dimensions separately if you serialize such a matrix, for example
      an empty kernel.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
